C LANGUAGE FUNDAMENTALS - QUICK REFERENCE
(Type | One-line explanation | One-line code)
================================================================

1. POINTERS
================================================================
Normal Pointer        | Stores the address of a variable.
                       | int *p = &x;

Pointer Arithmetic     | Moves by sizeof(type), not by 1 byte.
                       | p = p + 1;   // moves 4 bytes if int*

Function Pointer       | Stores the address of a function to call indirectly.
                       | int (*fp)(int, int) = add;

Pointer-to-Pointer     | A pointer that stores the address of another pointer.
                       | int **pp = &p;

Void Pointer           | Generic pointer that can hold any type's address.
                       | void *vp = &x;

NULL Pointer           | Pointer explicitly set to point to nothing.
                       | int *p = NULL;

Wild Pointer           | Uninitialized pointer pointing to a garbage address.
                       | int *p;   // uninitialized - wild pointer

Array Pointer          | A pointer to the first element / whole array.
                       | int *p = arr;   // arr decays to &arr[0]

Const Pointer          | Pointer whose address itself can't be changed.
                       | int *const p = &x;

Pointer to Const       | Pointer whose pointed-to value can't be modified.
                       | const int *p = &x;


2. MEMORY MANAGEMENT
================================================================
malloc                 | Allocates uninitialized heap memory.
                       | int *p = malloc(5 * sizeof(int));

calloc                 | Allocates zero-initialized heap memory.
                       | int *p = calloc(5, sizeof(int));

realloc                | Resizes a previously allocated memory block.
                       | p = realloc(p, 10 * sizeof(int));

free                   | Releases heap memory back to the system.
                       | free(p);

Memory Leak            | Allocated memory never freed, wasting resources.
                       | malloc(10);   // never freed - leak

Dangling Pointer       | Pointer still pointing to memory that's been freed.
                       | free(p); /* p is now dangling */

Double Free            | Calling free() twice on the same pointer - undefined behavior.
                       | free(p); free(p);   // BUG

Buffer Overflow        | Writing beyond the bounds of allocated memory.
                       | char buf[4]; strcpy(buf, "hello");  // overflow


3. STRUCTS, UNIONS, BIT-FIELDS
================================================================
Struct                 | Groups variables of different types under one name, each with separate memory.
                       | struct Point { int x, y; };

Union                  | All members share the same memory; only one is valid at a time.
                       | union Data { int i; float f; };

Bit-Field              | Packs a member into a specific number of bits inside an int.
                       | struct Flags { unsigned int isSet : 1; };

Nested Struct          | A struct containing another struct as a member.
                       | struct Line { struct Point a, b; };

Anonymous Union        | A union without a tag name, accessed directly by member.
                       | union { int i; float f; };   // members used directly

typedef struct         | Gives a struct a shorter alias name for reuse.
                       | typedef struct { int x; } Point;


4. VOLATILE, CONST, STATIC
================================================================
const (variable)       | Value cannot be modified after initialization.
                       | const int x = 5;

const pointer          | The pointer address is fixed, value can change.
                       | int *const p = &x;

pointer to const       | The value pointed to is fixed, pointer can change.
                       | const int *p = &x;

static (local var)     | Local variable retains its value between function calls.
                       | static int count = 0;

static (global var)    | Restricts visibility to the file it's declared in (internal linkage).
                       | static int fileOnly = 10;

static (function)      | Function is only callable within its own source file.
                       | static void helper(void) { }

volatile               | Tells compiler the value may change unexpectedly (signal/thread/hardware); disables optimization on it.
                       | volatile int flag = 0;


5. STACK vs HEAP
================================================================
Stack                  | Fast, automatically managed, limited size, scope-bound.
                       | int x = 5;   // stored on stack

Heap                   | Manually managed, larger, persists until freed.
                       | int *p = malloc(sizeof(int));   // stored on heap


6. UNDEFINED BEHAVIOR (UB) EXAMPLES
================================================================
Null Pointer Deref     | Dereferencing a NULL pointer.
                       | int *p = NULL; *p = 5;   // UB - crash

Array Out of Bounds    | Accessing an index outside the array's valid range.
                       | int arr[5]; arr[10] = 1;   // UB

Uninitialized Variable | Using a variable before it's assigned a value.
                       | int x; printf("%d", x);   // UB - garbage value

Signed Integer Overflow| Signed int exceeding its representable range.
                       | int x = INT_MAX; x = x + 1;   // UB

Division by Zero       | Dividing an integer by zero.
                       | int y = 10 / 0;   // UB - crash

Use After Free         | Accessing memory after it has been freed.
                       | free(p); printf("%d", *p);   // UB

