agent reads from that buffer. It's a producer-consumer pattern across
the kernel/userspace boundary, similar in spirit to IPC even though
it's not a traditional socket or pipe - the kernel is the producer,
userspace is the consumer."

Q: "What would happen if the userspace consumer falls behind the
kernel producer in the eBPF scanner?"
A: "The ring buffer would fill up, and depending on configuration,
either the oldest events get overwritten (data loss) or the kernel
side blocks/drops new events - so sizing the buffer and keeping the
userspace consumer fast enough not to bottleneck is a real design
concern, similar to backpressure handling in any producer-consumer
system."

CODE TALKING POINT - Unix domain socket (server side skeleton):
--------------------------------------------------------------
#include <sys/socket.h>
#include <sys/un.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int main(void) {
    int server_fd = socket(AF_UNIX, SOCK_STREAM, 0);
    struct sockaddr_un addr;
    memset(&addr, 0, sizeof(addr));
    addr.sun_family = AF_UNIX;
    strncpy(addr.sun_path, "/tmp/mydaemon.sock", sizeof(addr.sun_path) - 1);

    bind(server_fd, (struct sockaddr *)&addr, sizeof(addr));
    listen(server_fd, 5);

    int client_fd = accept(server_fd, NULL, NULL);
    char buf[256];
    ssize_t n = read(client_fd, buf, sizeof(buf) - 1);
    if (n > 0) {
        buf[n] = '\0';
        printf("Received: %s\n", buf);
    }

    close(client_fd);
    close(server_fd);
    return 0;
}

KEY POINT TO SAY: AF_UNIX + a filesystem path instead of an IP/port -
this is what makes it local-only IPC rather than network communication.


