#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>

void tail_f(const char *filename) {
    FILE *file = fopen(filename, "r");
    if (file == NULL) {
        perror("Error opening file");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    // Move to the end of the file to follow only new changes
    fseek(file, 0, SEEK_END);

    char buffer[1024];
    while (1) {
        // Read and print any new lines written to the file
        while (fgets(buffer, sizeof(buffer), file) != NULL) {
            printf("%s", buffer);
            fflush(stdout);
        }

        // Clear the EOF indicator so we can continue reading newly appended data
        clearerr(file);

        // Sleep briefly to prevent 100% CPU utilization
        usleep(100000); // 100 milliseconds
    }

    fclose(file);
}

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    if (argc < 2) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Usage: %s <filename>\n", argv[0]);
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    tail_f(argv[1]);
    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}

Core Keywords and Concepts
Prepared Statement (sqlite3_stmt*, sqlite3_prepare_v2)
What it is: A compiled representation of a SQL query stored in a binary form.
Why use it: It optimizes performance by parsing and compiling the SQL syntax only once, even if executed multiple times with different values. It also safely separates SQL code from user data.

Parameter Binding (sqlite3_bind_text, sqlite3_bind_int64)
What it is: The process of safely inserting variables into the placeholders (?) of a prepared statement.
Why use it: It completely prevents SQL Injection attacks because SQLite treats bound parameters strictly as data, never as executable code. It also handles automatic type mapping and character escaping.

Database Connection Pooling vs. Single Global Connection (static sqlite3* db)
What it is: Your code relies on a single global database pointer (static sqlite3* db) managed by helper functions, while several local functions (like getBaselineInfoForFile) open and close separate, localized connections to dbPath.
Why use it: Local connections open isolated database sessions for specific lookup tasks, whereas the global connection handles stateful insertions. An interviewer might ask about concurrency tradeoffs here (e.g., SQLite allows multiple concurrent readers, but only one writer at a time).

Mutex and Thread Safety (std::mutex, std::lock_guard)
What it is: A synchronization primitive used to protect shared data (db) from simultaneous access by multiple threads. std::lock_guard provides RAII-style locking (acquires the lock on creation and automatically releases it when it goes out of scope).
Why use it: SQLite is thread-safe by default (depending on compilation flags), but sharing a single connection pointer across multi-threaded operations without synchronization causes data corruption or crashes.

Memory Management Flags (SQLITE_STATIC vs. SQLITE_TRANSIENT)
What it is: Flags passed during parameter binding telling SQLite how to handle the memory of text strings.
Why use it: SQLITE_STATIC tells SQLite that the pointer will remain valid until the statement finishes executing (great for std::string::c_str() while the string is in scope). SQLITE_TRANSIENT forces SQLite to make its own internal copy of the data immediately (crucial if the source string might be modified or destroyed before the query runs).

RAII and Resource Cleanup (sqlite3_finalize, sqlite3_close)
What it is: Ensuring that system resources (statements, database handles) are properly released when they are no longer needed.
Why use it: Forgetting to call sqlite3_finalize on statements or sqlite3_close on connections causes severe memory leaks and can leave database files locked.

Raw String Literals (R"SQL(...)SQL")
What it is: A C++11 feature allowing multi-line strings without needing manual newline escapes (\n) or quote escaping.
Why use it: It makes complex, multi-line SQL queries clean, readable, and easy to maintain directly inside C++ source files.
